\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Claude Design shipped April 17 alongside Opus 4.7, in research preview. The coverage split cleanly. One camp: Figma\'92s stock dropped 7% and commentators argued about what that means for the design software industry. Another camp: the tool has real rough edges and commentators argued about whether those break the story. Both frames miss the point.\
Sometimes a product ships with warts and the warts don\'92t matter. Anthropic has a pattern of shipping those. Claude Code was the first. Cowork was the second. Claude Design is the third, the one that makes the strategy finally clear.\
All three do the same structural work: intent goes in as natural language, a working artifact comes out, and it hands off directly to the next product in the stack. With Design, the last big category of production work \'97 visual artifacts, the thing every product team makes first and shows to everyone \'97 joins Code and Cowork inside the same pipeline. The prototype isn\'92t an approximation of the thing anymore. It\'92s the thing, or one handoff away from it.\
Brilliant told Anthropic that its most intricate product pages used to require twenty or more prompts to recreate in competing tools. In Claude Design they need two. Datadog compressed a week-long cycle of briefs, mockups, and review rounds into a single conversation. Earlier this year a Jane Street designer wrote publicly that he now designs in Claude more than in Figma, building working prototypes directly in the codebase instead of making Figma mocks that describe what the code will eventually do. Those aren\'92t demos. They\'92re practitioners describing what happens when the mockup-to-production handoff \'97 the twenty-year-old ritual where designers make pixel approximations that engineers then rebuild in code \'97 gets replaced by one conversation that produces the actual UI in the medium it will run in. Most of how your team is structured was built around that ritual. It isn\'92t the ritual anymore.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

